WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.080
Eye-popping, thick-rimmed,&nbsp;
and instantly recognizable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:09.420
Bubbles’ glasses in the hit cult TV show&nbsp;
“Trailer Park Boys” are as tied to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.400
character as much as his love for cats. But&nbsp;
how did Bubbles acquire his signature look?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.220
"Trailer Park Boys" started as a grassroots&nbsp;
project that slowly grew into a cult classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.020
and despite its popularity, it still&nbsp;
checks many of the requisite boxes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.900
cult status. After the show became a hit on&nbsp;
Netflix, “Trailer Park Boys” garnered a loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.360
fanbase that fell in love with the likes&nbsp;
of Julian, Ricky, Bubbles, and Mr. Lahey.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.980
"Trailer Park Boys" may basically&nbsp;
be kind-of-sort-of mainstream now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.200
but no matter how many episodes the fans&nbsp;
consume, die-hards and first-timers alike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.820
may find themselves wondering one particular&nbsp;
question: What's up with Bubbles' glasses?

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.220
There's no doubt that the well-meaning Bubbles,&nbsp;
who is played by Mike Smith, is a massive breakout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:02.400
character. And those extra-thick Coke bottle&nbsp;
specs of his are almost too good to be true. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.960
where did the eyewear come from? Did the makers&nbsp;
of the show run against their lo-fi production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.760
values and commission the glasses? Or did&nbsp;
they luck out by finding the glasses that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.380
let's face it, look more or less like Bubbles&nbsp;
found them dirt cheap at a yard sale somewhere?

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:22.920
As luck would have it, the latter option actually&nbsp;
isn't too far from the iconic glasses' true origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.360
story. Here's how the makers of "Trailer&nbsp;
Park Boys" came up with Bubbles' glasses.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.120
Mike Clattenburg, the creator of "Trailer Park&nbsp;
Boys," told Sharp Magazine that he hired Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.360
Smith to do sound for the feature&nbsp;
film that gave birth to the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.840
which was shot in 1998. At his house, Smith put&nbsp;
on the infamous glasses and eased effortlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.000
into character. Clattenburg liked what&nbsp;
he saw, and as they say, a star was born.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.260
John Paul Tremblay, who plays Julian,&nbsp;
the muscle-bound trailer park resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.680
who wears black shirts and always&nbsp;
has a glass of rum and Coke in hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.940
is also enthused about the way Smith&nbsp;
introduced Bubbles to them. He said,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.360
"[Mike Smith] totally developed&nbsp;
that character. He threw those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.080
glasses on one day, and we were like,&nbsp;
Wow. This guy needs to be on the show.”

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:11.580
“I don’t know how you passed your eyetest&nbsp;
son, but you can’t see jack sh*t.”

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.940
In the very same interview, Smith&nbsp;
himself explained how he came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.580
possess those sweet frames. Smith explained,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.460
"My girlfriend at the time&nbsp;
was in Texas visiting family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:25.800
and they went to an estate sale and she found&nbsp;
these glasses. They were 50 cents, and she knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.700
that I would think they were funny because they&nbsp;
were so thick, so she bought them as a joke."

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.900
Showcase wasn't as convinced about Bubbles&nbsp;
as the makers of the show were at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.000
but Clattenburg eventually managed to persuade&nbsp;
the channel to include the character in the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.160
though he had to make a short film starring an&nbsp;
early version of Bubbles to do so. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.880
this was probably worth it, as&nbsp;
Clattenburg feels that "Trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.780
Park Boys" might not have succeeded at&nbsp;
all without the inclusion of Bubbles.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.940
While the glasses are no doubt an instrumental&nbsp;
part of what just might be the single most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.080
important character in "Trailer Park&nbsp;
Boys," Smith had to suffer for his art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.960
because Bubbles' signature eyewear is just about&nbsp;
as comfortable to use as you'd expect. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.680
he eventually learned to deal with&nbsp;
them. Smith told Big Issue in 2013,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.440
"They give you a proper whopping headache,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.100
but now when I put them on, my brain&nbsp;
turns off my eye pain somehow."

